  "conclusion": "Plan B is provisionally preferable because it secures a significant rescue (cooling twelve households) without violating any individual’s stronger negative rights. Plan A directly violates Mira’s foundational right to a home; Plan C violates Sal’s income right, which still outweighs the additional individual rescue to Jo when some rescue is already possible without that violation.",
  "strongest_objection": "Jo endures the greatest heat exposure; Plan C uniquely relieves that extreme risk.",
  "answer_to_objection": "The council’s stronger duty not to wrongfully strip Sal of his livelihood prevails over the weaker positive duty to relieve Jo once a rescue option (Plan B) exists that respects all comparable rights.",
  "reversal_condition": "If Jo’s heat exposure rose to an immediate life-threat level while Sal could survive eight weeks without catastrophic loss, the priority between duties might invert, favoring Plan C.",
  "residual_disagreement": [
    "Whether uncompensated eight-week closure is less severe than homelessness",
    "Whether positive rescue for extreme heat should override livelihood in emergencies"
  ]
}
